# Cloudflare Pages redirects — 3locksmiths.co.il
#
# Ships in out/ from public/ (next build copies public/ wholesale; verified: out/_headers is
# byte-identical to public/_headers). wrangler auto-detects it at the root of the deploy dir —
# ops/deploy-site.ps1 does NOT special-case it, line 255 is a plain `npx wrangler pages deploy`.
#
# Format: <from>  <to>  <status>. First match wins.
#
# ── THREE THINGS THAT MAKE A RULE HERE SILENTLY DO NOTHING ────────────────────
#
# 1. CASE. Rule matching is a case-SENSITIVE byte comparison of the percent-encoded request path
#    — verified 2026-09-02: galbath.co.il/public/_redirects:40 holds an UPPERCASE rule; the
#    uppercase request 301s and its byte-identical lowercase twin 404s.
#
#    This site emits LOWERCASE %d7% everywhere — sitemap.xml, every canonical, every authored
#    href — so lowercase is what Google indexed and the only form that must match. Uppercase is
#    what encodeURI() and most editors produce, so an uppercase-only rule set would be a 100%
#    silent no-op against every real URL.
#
#    The uppercase lines below are NOT insurance against Cloudflare uppercase-normalising these
#    rules at parse time. If it does that, the lowercase lines are dead and real traffic 404s
#    regardless of what the uppercase lines say. They cover exactly one thing: a third-party
#    backlink that re-encoded the path in uppercase. No sibling site has a lowercase rule, so
#    the lowercase form has no prior art anywhere in the fleet — hence the VERIFY block below.
#
#    Do NOT copy the raw-Hebrew idiom from dalita.co.il/public/_redirects:11 — Cloudflare
#    normalises a raw rule to UPPERCASE, so that file matches only uppercase requests and is
#    half-broken live today. It also refutes galbath's own comment at :19.
#
# 2. TRAILING SLASH. A rule written with a slash does not match a request without one. Today the
#    no-slash form 308s, and its Location header carries RAW unencoded Hebrew — a response no
#    _redirects rule here could produce, which proves it is Pages' own directory resolution. It
#    dies the moment the directory leaves out/, turning a working URL into a hard 404. Hence
#    both forms for every path.
#
# 3. NO WILDCARDS UNDER /locations/. _redirects outranks existing static assets on Pages
#    (verified on thermoleak.co.il: /404/ redirects although out/404/index.html exists), so a
#    convenient `/locations/*` catch-all would black-hole all 25 surviving city pages. Exact
#    paths only. (410 is reportedly not expressible on Pages — UNVERIFIED, repeated from
#    galbath _redirects:33. Irrelevant here: 301 is correct regardless.)
#
# ── WHY THESE SEVEN ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Seven location pages withdrawn 2026-09-02 because the business no longer services those
# cities (docs/business-facts.md §E.1). באר שבע and ירושלים are original pre-migration
# WordPress permalinks holding real ranking signal, which is what CLAUDE.md golden rule 5 is
# about; the other five were generated by this project and are redirected too because they were
# live and in the sitemap.
#
# Target is the service-areas hub, not the homepage: a visitor who searched for a specific city
# can self-select one that is actually served, and the hub no longer claims the withdrawn ones.
# Not a nearest-city map — there is no honest substitute for ירושלים or באר שבע among the
# survivors, and Google treats a redirect to a non-equivalent page as a soft 404 anyway.
#
# VERIFY AFTER EVERY DEPLOY. This is the only gate: ops/deploy-site.ps1 asserts nothing about
# this file, so `ls out/_redirects` plus the curl below is the whole check.
#
#   curl -sSI 'https://3locksmiths.co.il/locations/%d7%a9%d7%9b%d7%a4%d7%95%d7%9c-%d7%9e%d7%a4%d7%aa%d7%97-%d7%9b%d7%a8%d7%9e%d7%99%d7%90%d7%9c/' | grep -iE 'HTTP/|location'
#   expect: HTTP/2 301  +  location: /%d7%90%d7%96%d7%95%d7%a8%d7%99-%d7%a9%d7%99%d7%a8%d7%95%d7%aa/
#
# A 404 there means the lowercase rules were normalised away at parse time and the only lever
# left is a zone-level Cloudflare Redirect Rule. The test is meaningful because asset lookup on
# this site is ALSO case-sensitive — verified 2026-09-02 on a surviving page: the lowercase path
# returns 200 and its uppercase twin returns 404 — so a 301 on the lowercase form can only have
# come from a lowercase rule matching.

